Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Our experimental plan aims to reproduce and extend the “Conformal Prediction as Bayesian Quadrature” framework by implementing three conformal decision rules—Split Conformal Prediction (SCP), Conformal Risk Control (CRC), and a Bayesian quadrature-based high posterior density (HPD) rule employing a Dirichlet quantile-spacing bound—to maximize the reliability and tightness of data-conditional, distribution‐free risk guarantees.

1. Overview:
   • Objective: For a given bounded (B=1) and monotone-in-λ loss, identify the minimal candidate λ such that the posterior probability Pr(L+ ≤ α) (computed via the Bayesian quadrature formulation) exceeds a confidence threshold β (e.g., β=0.95). 
   • Key innovation: Use Monte Carlo Dirichlet sampling with U ~ Dir(1,…,1) to approximate the posterior distribution of L+ = ΣUᵢ ℓ(i) (with ℓ(n+1)=1), and compare three decision rules:
       – SCP: Recovering the standard s-order statistic for miscoverage loss.
       – CRC: Utilizing the posterior mean E(L+) = (Σ ℓᵢ + 1)/(n+1), thus recovering the classical risk control.
       – HPD Rule: Selecting λ via a novel Monte Carlo estimate ensuring Pr(L+ ≤ α) ≥ β.

2. Experimental Suites:
   A. Synthetic Binomial Loss:
      • Data Generation: Generate a synthetic binomial dataset where the true risk is known (e.g., true risk = 1–λ). For each candidate λ, compute individual losses ℓᵢ(λ) for n trials.
      • Procedure:
         – For each candidate λ, sort ℓᵢ and set ℓ(n+1)=1.
         – Perform Monte Carlo sampling (e.g., 1000 iterations) from Dir(1,…,1) to compute L+ per trial.
         – Determine the minimal λ for which the empirical estimate Pr(L+ ≤ α) ≥ β, and record per-trial failure rates (fraction of splits exceeding α) and utility metrics (set sizes).
      • Ablation: Vary n, candidate grid resolution (K), α, and β values (β ∈ {0.80, 0.90, 0.95, 0.99}) to assess sensitivity of decisions.

   B. Synthetic Heteroskedastic Regression:
      • Data Generation: Simulate a regression dataset with heteroskedastic noise, where the miscoverage loss (indicator function of whether the true output falls outside the prediction interval) is measured.
      • Procedure:
         – Use a simple regression model to generate predictions along with a set of candidate intervals.
         – Compute sample-specific losses ℓᵢ(λ) and apply the Bayesian quadrature-based framework to select λ.
         – Evaluate reliability (failure rate) vs. utility (average prediction interval length) across different noise and heteroskedasticity levels.

   C. MS-COCO Multilabel Classification (Control of False Negative Rate):
      • Data: Use a subset of the MS-COCO dataset. For each image, obtain multilabel scores using a pretrained convolutional neural network (e.g., fine-tuned ResNet using PyTorch/torchvision).
      • Procedure:
         – For each image prediction, determine candidate decision thresholds λ that affect the constructed prediction sets.
         – Here, the loss ℓᵢ(λ) is defined based on the false negative rate (FNR), with B=1.
         – Apply the three decision rules to calibrate predictions such that the empirical FNR across random splits remains controlled below α, with posterior guarantee probability exceeding β.
         – Measure both per-trial failure rate and prediction set tightness (average set size).

3. Implementation Details:
   • Loss Computation: For a candidate λ, calculate individual losses ℓᵢ(λ) ensuring their values lie in [0, 1] and are monotonic.
   • Bayesian Quadrature Mapping: For each λ, sort ℓᵢ, append ℓ(n+1)=1, then use Monte Carlo simulations to compute the distribution of L+ = Σ Uᵢ ℓ(i) where U ~ Dir(1,…,1). Approve λ if Pr(L+ ≤ α) ≥ β.
   • Tools/Environment: Use Python (≥3.10) with libraries numpy, scipy, and PyTorch (torchvision for COCO). Ensure experiments are reproducible by fixing random seeds and deterministic splits.
   • Code Structure: Develop modular routines for each decision rule and loss simulation. Use Jupyter or scripted notebooks for rapid prototyping and systematic logging (with version tracking via Git).
   • GPU Acceleration: Leverage GPU (when available) in the MS-COCO experiments to speed up CNN inference.

4. Evaluation Metrics:
   • Primary Evaluation: Per-trial failure rate (fraction of splits where true risk exceeds α, aiming for a β threshold) and utility metrics (e.g., prediction interval length for regression or prediction set size for multilabel classification).
   • Secondary Analysis: Ablations on the number of splits, candidate grid resolution K, and sensitivity analysis over different β values to understand conservative vs. aggressive decisions.

5. Expected Contributions:
   • Demonstrate that the Bayesian quadrature-based HPD rule yields tighter risk guarantees and more efficient prediction sets compared to classical SCP and CRC.
   • Validate that CRC corresponds to using the posterior mean, and that SCP is recovered in standard miscoverage loss settings.
   • Provide thorough sensitivity analyses and reproducible benchmarks that will inform future high-stakes applications in uncertainty quantification.

This plan integrates ideas from the provided literature, leverages Bayesian integration techniques, and sets forth a clear, reproducible, and innovative experimental agenda combining synthetic and real-world tasks.
PLAN
``` 
