Reviews: Reviewer #1:
(6.840909090909088, 'The performance of your submission is: 6.840909090909088THOUGHT:\nThe paper presents an interesting idea by leveraging Bayesian quadrature to reformulate conformal prediction and deploy a novel HPD rule alongside standard methods (SCP, CRC, RCPS) for risk control. The experiments are detailed and the methodology is well articulated, with thorough discussions of both synthetic and simulated multilabel classification settings. However, several issues temper my enthusiasm. First, the reliance on synthetic experiments and simulated MS-COCO data raises concerns about the method’s practical applicability and reproducibility in real-world, complex datasets. Second, the method depends heavily on the i.i.d. and bounded loss assumptions which may not hold in many realistic scenarios, and the paper does not provide sufficient discussion or experiments addressing these limitations beyond acknowledging them. Third, while the integration of full posterior sampling is novel, the computational costs and sensitivity to hyperparameters such as the candidate grid resolution are not deeply analyzed. Finally, although the paper recovers classical methods as special cases, the added benefit of the HPD rule over simpler alternatives is not convincingly demonstrated through extensive ablation or comparison on diverse datasets. Taken together, despite its strengths in exposition and a promising research direction, the practical impact and robustness of the proposed approach remain uncertain. I lean toward rejecting the paper in its current form, though I encourage the authors to address these concerns and provide more compelling real-world validation.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper introduces a Bayesian quadrature framework for conformal prediction to enforce data-conditional, distribution-free risk guarantees. By formulating an aggregated loss using Dirichlet sampling, it recovers classical methods (SCP, CRC) and proposes a novel high posterior density (HPD) rule to select decision thresholds. The method is evaluated on synthetic binomial loss, heteroskedastic regression, and simulated MS-COCO multilabel classification tasks, showing that the HPD rule can achieve lower failure rates with competitive utility compared to baselines.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "Innovative combination of Bayesian quadrature and conformal prediction.",\n    "Comprehensive experimental description covering multiple synthetic setups.",\n    "Clear attempt to recover classical methods as special cases and extend them with the HPD rule.",\n    "Detailed presentation with theoretical derivations and discussions of decision rules."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "Heavy reliance on synthetic experiments and simulated data limits demonstration of practical applicability.",\n    "Assumptions such as i.i.d. calibration losses and bounded loss may not hold in many real-world scenarios.",\n    "Limited discussion on computational efficiency and sensitivity to hyperparameter choices like candidate grid resolution.",\n    "The added benefit of the HPD rule over traditional methods is not fully substantiated by diverse real-world benchmarks.",\n    "The impact of model mis-specification and non-i.i.d. settings is acknowledged but not adequately addressed with experiments."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 3,\n  "Quality": 3,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 3,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Can the authors provide experimental validation on real-world datasets beyond synthetic settings to demonstrate the practical effectiveness of the method?",\n    "How sensitive is the HPD rule to the choice of candidate grid resolution and other hyperparameters, and are there guidelines for setting these in practice?",\n    "Have the authors considered the computational overhead of the Monte Carlo Dirichlet sampling, especially in large-scale or real-time applications?",\n    "Can the method be extended or adapted to settings where the i.i.d. assumption for calibration losses is violated, and what would be the expected impact on risk guarantees?"\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The reliance on i.i.d. and bounded loss assumptions may limit the method\'s applicability in scenarios with heavy-tailed or dependent data.",\n    "Limited real-world experimental validation may hinder the assessment of robustness in practical high-stakes applications.",\n    "The computational cost of full posterior sampling needs further discussion, especially in large-scale settings."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 3,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 3,\n  "Overall": 6,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True), 
Reviewer #2:
(6.386363636363633, 'The performance of your submission is: 6.386363636363633THOUGHT:\nThe paper proposes an innovative approach that recasts conformal prediction by using Bayesian quadrature with Monte Carlo Dirichlet sampling to control risk. The work aims to recover standard methods (SCP and CRC) while introducing an HPD rule that accounts for the full posterior distribution of an aggregated loss quantity L⁺. My review acknowledges the technical sophistication and potential impact of merging Bayesian integration with conformity guarantees. However, I have serious reservations regarding its heavy reliance on synthetic experiments, the bounded loss and i.i.d. assumptions, and practical scalability issues. Moreover, while the derivations are solid, there remain open questions about robustness in non-i.i.d./real-world settings, clarity regarding candidate grid resolution effects, and the computational overhead. In summary, although the idea is interesting and could be impactful if extended further, the current paper lacks sufficient empirical validation and discussion of limitations that are critical for high-stakes applications.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "This paper introduces a novel framework that integrates Bayesian quadrature with conformal prediction in order to achieve rigorous, data-conditional, distribution-free risk guarantees. By constructing an aggregated loss L⁺ using Monte Carlo Dirichlet sampling, the method aims to select a decision threshold λ that ensures Pr(L⁺ ≤ α) ≥ β. The paper shows that standard methods such as Split Conformal Prediction (SCP) and Conformal Risk Control (CRC) are recovered as special cases, and it proposes a new High Posterior Density (HPD) rule that offers improved risk control. Experimental results on synthetic binomial loss, heteroskedastic regression, and a multilabel classification mimic on MS-COCO are provided, along with sensitivity analyses on hyperparameters.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "Proposes an interesting and novel combination of Bayesian quadrature and conformal prediction.",\n    "Provides a unifying framework that recovers existing methods while introducing a new HPD decision rule.",\n    "Extensive theoretical exposition accompanied by detailed derivations that are well integrated into decision thresholds.",\n    "Includes comprehensive synthetic experiments and sensitivity analyses on hyperparameters like confidence levels and grid resolution."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "Heavy reliance on synthetic experiments; the real-world applicability, especially on genuine complex datasets, remains unproven.",\n    "Assumes bounded losses and i.i.d. calibration data, which may limit the method\'s generalizability to practical settings with heavy tails or dependent data.",\n    "The computational overhead due to Monte Carlo Dirichlet sampling could be prohibitive in large-scale or real-time applications.",\n    "Some parts of the paper are overly technical, which may hinder reproducibility and practical interpretation without more accessible empirical guidelines."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 3,\n  "Quality": 3,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 3,\n  "Questions": [\n    "How sensitive is the proposed HPD rule to the choice of candidate grid resolution, and can an adaptive mechanism be designed to address this?",\n    "Can the authors provide more discussion or preliminary results on how the method performs under non-i.i.d. settings or with heavy-tailed loss distributions?",\n    "What are the computational trade-offs in real large-scale applications, and how might the approach be scaled (e.g., via GPU acceleration or parallelization) in practice?",\n    "Could the authors clarify any potential limitations when integrating these decision rules within end-to-end predictive systems?"\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The framework relies on the i.i.d. and bounded loss assumptions, which may not hold in many real-world applications.",\n    "Empirical validation is largely conducted on synthetic or mimicked datasets rather than on real-world high-stakes scenarios.",\n    "Potential high computational costs due to Monte Carlo sampling are not fully addressed.",\n    "The trade-off between risk control and utility (tightness of prediction sets) may require further practical tuning."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 3,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 3,\n  "Overall": 5,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True), 
Reviewer #3:
(7.409090909090907, 'The performance of your submission is: 7.409090909090907THOUGHT:\nThe paper proposes a framework that reformulates conformal prediction using Bayesian quadrature. It recovers known methods (SCP, CRC) and introduces a high posterior density (HPD) rule using Monte Carlo Dirichlet sampling. The paper is well structured with abstract, introduction, methods, experiments, results, and discussion. Its strengths include a clear, modular presentation; solid derivations; and comprehensive synthetic experiments that showcase how the new HPD rule can yield tighter risk control compared to baselines. However, there are weaknesses: the experimental validation primarily relies on synthetic data (with the multilabel classification being simulated rather than run on the full real dataset), and there is a reliance on assumptions such as i.i.d. and bounded losses that might limit applicability in more complex real-world scenarios. Additionally, while the theoretical grounding is solid, further discussion on sensitivity to hyperparameters and computational scalability would be beneficial. Overall, I view the contribution as novel and interesting, though further real-world validations would strengthen the work. I lean towards an Accept decision given its clear innovation and solid execution, with suggestions to address the identified limitations in future iterations.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper presents a novel framework that integrates Bayesian quadrature into conformal prediction for risk control. By framing the aggregated loss using Dirichlet-distributed weights and proposing a high posterior density (HPD) rule, the method recovers classical approaches (SCP and CRC) while providing tighter risk guarantees. The approach is evaluated on synthetic binomial loss, heteroskedastic regression, and simulated multilabel classification, showing improvements in risk control and utility over baseline methods.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "Innovative integration of Bayesian quadrature with conformal prediction.",\n    "Comprehensive derivation that recovers standard methods as special cases.",\n    "Clear experimental setup with synthetic tasks demonstrating improved risk control.",\n    "Well-structured paper with all necessary sections and thorough discussion."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "The experiments rely heavily on synthetic datasets; real-world validation is limited.",\n    "Assumptions such as i.i.d. calibration losses and bounded loss may restrict applicability.",\n    "Sensitivity to hyperparameter choices (e.g., grid resolution, Monte Carlo iterations) is not deeply explored.",\n    "Scalability and computational efficiency, especially for large-scale real datasets, are not fully addressed."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 3,\n  "Quality": 3,\n  "Clarity": 4,\n  "Significance": 3,\n  "Questions": [\n    "How does the HPD rule perform on fully real-world datasets beyond the synthetic experiments?",\n    "Can the authors provide further insights or sensitivity analyses on the chosen hyperparameters, such as the candidate grid resolution and the number of Monte Carlo iterations?",\n    "How might the method be adapted for settings where the i.i.d. assumption or bounded loss condition does not hold?",\n    "What computational improvements or scalability considerations could be integrated to handle large-scale datasets in practice?"\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "Heavy reliance on synthetic experiments limits direct evidence of real-world performance.",\n    "Assumptions of i.i.d. and bounded loss might make the method overly conservative in non-ideal scenarios.",\n    "Potential increased computational cost due to Monte Carlo Dirichlet sampling, which may be challenging for large datasets."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 3,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 3,\n  "Overall": 7,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Accept"\n}\n```', True)
